1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Today we compare two synchronization tools that look similar but solve different problems: the mutex and the semaphore. Both exist to protect shared data when several threads run at once.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Start with the problem. Two threads increment the same counter. Each reads the value, adds one and writes it back. If they interleave, one update is lost. That is a race condition in the critical section.

3
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:22,000
A mutex, short for mutual exclusion, is a lock with one rule: only one thread can hold it at a time. A thread locks before entering the critical section and unlocks when it leaves.

4
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:50,000
A mutex has an owner. The thread that locked it is the only one that should unlock it. This ownership is the main conceptual difference from a semaphore.

5
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:18,000
A semaphore is an integer counter with two atomic operations. Wait decrements the counter and blocks the caller if the result would go below zero. Signal increments the counter and wakes a waiting thread.

6
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:46,000
A binary semaphore has the values zero and one and behaves like a lock. A counting semaphore starts at n and lets up to n threads through, which is useful for a pool of identical resources like database connections.

7
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:14,000
A semaphore has no owner. One thread can wait and a completely different thread can signal. That makes semaphores a signalling mechanism, not just a lock.

8
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:42,000
The producer consumer problem shows this. The producer signals a semaphore when it adds an item to the buffer, and the consumer waits on that semaphore before taking one. Two different threads, one shared counter.

9
00:03:46,000 --> 00:04:10,000
So the rule of thumb: use a mutex to protect a critical section, and use a semaphore to coordinate threads or to limit access to a counted resource.

10
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Both can cause trouble. If a thread locks a mutex and never unlocks it, every other thread waits forever. Forgetting to signal a semaphore has the same effect.

11
00:04:42,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Deadlock appears when two threads each hold one lock and wait for the other. Thread one holds lock A and wants B, thread two holds B and wants A. Neither can proceed, which is exactly the Coffman circular wait.

12
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:34,000
The standard prevention is a global lock ordering. If every thread always acquires lock A before lock B, a circular wait cannot form and the deadlock is impossible.

13
00:05:38,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Another hazard is priority inversion, where a low priority thread holds a mutex that a high priority thread needs, and a medium priority thread keeps the processor busy. Priority inheritance is the usual fix.

14
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Operating systems implement both with an atomic hardware instruction such as test and set or compare and swap, and with a wait queue so blocked threads sleep instead of spinning.

15
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:58,000
To summarize: a mutex is an owned lock for mutual exclusion, a semaphore is a counter for signalling and counted resources, and careless use of either can lead to deadlock.
